rShapeAnnotation:","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/org.swift.MyProject\/documentation\/MapLibre\/MLNMapViewDelegate\/mapView:lineWidthForPolylineAnnotation:"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The vertices are automatically connected in the order in which you provide","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"them. The first and last vertices are not connected to each other, but you can","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"specify the same ","type":"text"},{"code":"CLLocationCoordinate2D","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" as the first and last vertices in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order to close the polyline. To fill the space within the shape, use an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/org.swift.MyProject\/documentation\/MapLibre\/MLNPolygon","type":"reference"},{"text":" object. To group multiple polylines together in one shape, use an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/org.swift.MyProject\/documentation\/MapLibre\/MLNMultiPolyline","type":"reference"},{"text":" or ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/org.swift.MyProject\/documentation\/MapLibre\/MLNShapeCollection","type":"reference"},{"text":" object.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"To make the polyline go across the antimeridian or international date line,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"specify some longitudes less than −180 degrees or greater than 180 degrees.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"For example, a polyline that stretches from Tokyo to San Francisco would have","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"coordinates of (35.68476, -220.24257) and (37.78428, -122.41310).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","sy